# Public-release safety and licensing audit

**Audit date:** 2026-10-13

**Repository:** `1337isnot1337/e-99` (observed as **private**)

**Inspected baseline:** `origin/main` at `ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc`

**Theorem state:** `2026-10-10.audit.234`; Level I open, Targets A/B open, Target C-I closed and C-II open. Target A is canonical through Section 177.

This is a conservative preparation audit, not a clearance opinion. No file is
approved for public distribution by this document. The inventory utility is
read-only and dry-run only: it reports committed paths and Git blob IDs but does
not copy, redact, or publish content. Its `include` action means *candidate after
all global license, owner, and content-review gates*, not permission to redistribute.

## Findings

- There is no tracked root `LICENSE`, `COPYING`, or `NOTICE` file and no recorded
  contributor license agreement or DCO process. The repository is private. Do not
  change visibility based on this audit.
- `papers/` contains 30 PDFs: seven supplied primary papers, 20 related papers,
  and three supplementary papers/theses. `extracted/` contains 25 page images
  and extracted text derived from papers. These are third-party material, not
  project-owned assets by default.
- The related-literature README says that some source records identify open
  licenses but expressly makes no blanket redistribution claim. The annotated
  review identifies at least one paper under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. A checksum or a
  citation does not grant distribution rights. No PDF-by-PDF rights register in
  the repository establishes that every bundled copy and extraction can be
  redistributed. Exclude all papers and derived page images/text from a first
  curated export until a rights reviewer records the applicable license or
  permission for each file. Prefer a bibliographic citation and a link to an
  authorized source when no redistributable copy is established.
- The checkout has 4,579 tracked files and is about 213 MB. It includes 1,096
  files under `docs/reports/`, 313 under `docs/audits/`, and four under
  `docs/archive/`. These records are valuable private provenance, but their
  presence on `main` does not establish publication consent, authorship, or
  canonical status. Retain them in this private repository. The integrated
  [dependency planner](RELEASE_PREPARATION.md) identifies the exact evidence required
  by canonical claims and task cards; those files are review candidates regardless
  of this historical blanket exclusion. No essential evidence may be dropped from
  an exported research task merely to simplify clearance.
- The reachable history contains 6,014 commits; the local object database has
  42,614 objects and `git log --all` reports nine distinct author name/email
  pairs. Git history also carries earlier versions of research and binary
  material. A clean-history export to a separately approved repository is safer
  than changing visibility on this archive. Build that export from an allow-listed committed tree only after
  content, license, attribution, and secret review. Do not rewrite this history.
- The checked-out path scan found no files named like credentials, private keys,
  or environment-secret files. A limited local regex pass inspected all 42,614
  Git objects, including unreferenced objects, and scanned 13,828 text blobs.
  It found no PEM private-key markers or common AWS, GitHub, or Slack token
  shapes; it counted 36 email-shaped strings without printing their values.
  Those strings may be public citations or private identities and require
  review. This is not a comprehensive secret or personal-data scan. No dedicated
  Gitleaks, TruffleHog, or detect-secrets executable was available during this
  audit. Run a maintained scanner with redacted output before any release. Do
  not copy scanner findings or values into public issues, commits, or reports.

## Dry-run file disposition

Run the inventory against the exact committed revision intended for review:

```sh
python3 tools/publication_inventory.py --ref HEAD --summary
python3 tools/publication_inventory.py --ref HEAD > /tmp/e99-publication-inventory.json
```

The full JSON lists every tracked path, its action, reason, byte size, and Git
blob ID. It never emits file contents. By default, only established repository
infrastructure is marked as a candidate include; that candidate status is
blocked until the project has an owner-approved license and all applicable
reviews. The conservative rules are:

| Inventory action | Default treatment | Main path groups |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `include` | Candidate infrastructure only; global license and owner-approval gates remain | Root README and basic project metadata; `tools/`; `scripts/` |
| `exclude` | Keep out of the first export; reconsider only after specific clearance | `papers/`, `extracted/`, `docs/archive/`, `docs/reports/`, `docs/audits/`, historical prompts, legacy/cleanup research, generated artifact index, and binary content such as PDFs/images |
| `review` | No export decision until a person reviews the file and its provenance | Active theorem state and route maps, current prompts, `research/`, `AGENTS.md`, `.github/`, and all other unmatched files |

On the inspected baseline, the tool classified **14** paths as infrastructure
candidates, **1,487** as default exclusions, and **3,078** for human review. These
counts are a reproducibility aid, not a launch quota; the exact path list and blob
IDs come from the JSON output.

The inventory reads the requested Git commit, not uncommitted working-tree
changes. Re-run it after the proposed export tree is committed. A report generated
from an older base commit must not be used as a file list for a later export.

## Rights, ownership, and attribution model

Keep these decisions separate and explicit:

1. **Code.** The repository owner must choose and approve a software license
   before code is advertised as reusable. Do not assume that a license for code
   also covers prose, theorem writeups, scans, or derived text.
2. **Research text and contributions.** The owner should select a clear license
   for project-authored mathematical exposition and define how new contributors
   grant rights to their contributions. Preserve contributor-supplied attribution
   and provenance; do not infer mathematical authorship from commit authorship
   or AI-assistance metadata.
3. **Third-party publications.** Track each PDF, extracted page image, and
   substantial text extraction separately with bibliographic identity, exact
   source URL, applicable license or written permission, required attribution,
   and redistribution scope. Until then, omit the binary and derived extraction;
   keep citation metadata only after its own review.
4. **Attribution.** Record human contributions and requested names/handles with
   consent. AI-agent review is the project's sole mathematical review mechanism, with
   one independently operated agent audit required for consequential canonical
   promotion. No human mathematical review is expected. This does not settle
   authorship or third-party redistribution rights; record source credits
   and proof lifecycle separately.

These are recommendations for an owner decision, not a selected or applied
license. No license file was added by this change.

## Prelaunch blocking checklist

- [ ] Owner chooses a code license and a separate policy for mathematical text,
      contributions, and AI-assisted material; license files and contribution
      terms are reviewed before publishing.
- [ ] A maintainer creates a versioned, file-level allow-list from the final
      export candidate and signs off on every `review` item. `exclude` defaults
      remain in force until a documented exception is approved.
- [ ] Every PDF and extracted derivative has a verified redistribution basis,
      including license version, noncommercial/share-alike conditions, required
      notices, and source/author attribution. Otherwise remove it from the export
      tree and point to an authorized copy.
- [ ] A current secret and personal-data scan covers the export tree and the
      history that will actually be published. Findings are handled privately;
      scanner output is redacted.
- [ ] The release is built in a separate repository from an allow-listed tree
      with a new initial history, after preserving the private source repository.
      Review commit identities, branches, tags, generated files, fixtures, and
      CI logs for unintended disclosure.
- [ ] Active theorem state, links, third-party citations, contributor credits,
      and generated files are reviewed against the exact export commit. Public
      wording must continue to state that E99 is open.
- [ ] Repository owner explicitly approves any visibility change or new public
      destination. This audit authorizes neither action.

## Verification boundaries

The baseline, file counts, remote visibility, and available scanner executables
were checked locally on the date above. The inventory and its tests verify
classification mechanics and enumerate the chosen committed tree; the limited
regex sweep does not verify a license or detect all secrets or personal
information. This work did not create a publishable export. Theorem-state
consistency is unchanged.

## Integration correction

The historical counts and scan above remain dated observations. The current
[dependency-closed preparation process](RELEASE_PREPARATION.md) takes precedence over
blanket archive exclusions when constructing a usable release plan. Required reports
and audits remain pending exact-blob review. No scan result or inventory classification
is legal clearance or contributor consent. Publication and a redistributable export
remain blocked by the owner/file-review gates.

## Owner decision register (2026-10-15)

These are alternatives for review, not selected terms. A root license cannot grant
rights the project does not hold, clear earlier contributions, or license third-party
material. Generated indexes and AI-assisted text need the same source/attribution review
as their inputs; mechanical regeneration supplies no ownership determination.

| Decision | Concrete alternatives and consequences |
| --- | --- |
| Software | [MIT](https://opensource.org/license/mit) permits broad reuse with retained notices. [Apache-2.0](https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0) adds an express contributor patent grant, modification notices and applicable NOTICE obligations. [GPL-3.0](https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.html) requires corresponding source and license compliance when covered modified software is conveyed. Choose the intended reuse model and verify all included contributions can use it. |
| Project mathematical exposition | [CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/) permits reuse and adaptations with attribution and change indication. [CC BY-SA 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/) also requires shared adaptations to use a compatible ShareAlike license. Retaining present restrictions keeps public reuse blocked. Define file scope separately from software and third-party quotations. |
| Contributions | Explicit inbound terms matching the selected file license, possibly with a [DCO attestation](https://developercertificate.org/), keep a rights/provenance record. A separately reviewed CLA could provide a different grant. Neither mechanism retroactively establishes earlier consent; obtain it from actual rights holders. |
| Attribution and privacy | Agree on public names/handles and credit with contributors; review exact retained text privately. Keep reviewer details and approval records outside the export. An email count, Git author identity or AI disclosure does not establish consent or mathematical authorship. |
| Third-party material | Keep bibliography links and omit unlicensed source copies/derivatives, or obtain and record a specific redistribution basis and required notices. Current PDF/extraction exclusion stays in force. Text quotations inside retained reports still need review. |
| Destination and history | Approve a separate curated repository and its first clean commit after clearance. Keep this complete historical repository private. No visibility change, public tag or release is authorized by a passing validator. |
| Governance | The project owner operates one independent AI-agent mathematical audit for consequential theorem promotions, combining full proof reconstruction and adversarial bridge/regression checks at the exact SHA. No human mathematical referee or second review is required. Protect the future destination with PR/integrity checks after hosted checks can execute. Release ownership and rights/privacy clearance remain separate. |

The immediate human bottleneck is the exact-blob rights/attribution/privacy review of
the retained evidence, together with the software/text licensing decision. Do not
reduce the evidence set merely to avoid those reviews. The file plan exposes each
pending approval field; there are no actual owner or file approvals in the template.
